and then Ubered from the gym to here

00:45:57.660 --> 00:46:00.240
and so I had two different
drivers this morning

00:46:00.240 --> 00:46:03.851
and driver number one,
I sat in the front seat

00:46:03.851 --> 00:46:06.550
'cause he had a very
small car and I was like,

00:46:06.550 --> 00:46:08.930
sometimes in the backseat with me,

00:46:08.930 --> 00:46:10.720
my head will hit the ceiling.

00:46:10.720 --> 00:46:12.500
Yeah you got--
Most cars actually.

00:46:12.500 --> 00:46:14.040
Got your chin on your knees.

00:46:14.040 --> 00:46:19.040
And you know, there was some
small talk about the weather

00:46:20.720 --> 00:46:23.300
and I was like is this gonna
turn into a conversation?

00:46:23.300 --> 00:46:24.133
Mm.

00:46:25.622 --> 00:46:27.250
Of course I'm hoping not.

00:46:27.250 --> 00:46:29.890
He brought up the weather
'cause you're in the front seat.

00:46:29.890 --> 00:46:31.360
Yeah I know, I'm inviting this.

00:46:31.360 --> 00:46:33.420
But I was told that the app will now,

00:46:33.420 --> 00:46:36.680
you can indicate on the app
if you're a conversationalist

00:46:36.680 --> 00:46:39.440
or not and I know I said that--

00:46:39.440 --> 00:46:40.890
I haven't looked into that.

00:46:42.010 --> 00:46:43.023
You said you're not.

00:46:44.770 --> 00:46:46.100
What?
A conversationalist.

00:46:46.100 --> 00:46:48.570
I was told that that option exists

00:46:48.570 --> 00:46:49.403
but I have not seen it either.

00:46:49.403 --> 00:46:50.980
I don't know any of the options.

00:46:50.980 --> 00:46:52.881
So I ended up just kinda
getting on my phone

00:46:52.881 --> 00:46:56.433
and then I start thinking, again,

00:46:57.500 --> 00:46:59.550
I feel like if you look at a pie chart

00:46:59.550 --> 00:47:01.303
of my brain activity at this point,

00:47:05.936 --> 00:47:10.360
at least 10%, sometimes
revving up to 30 to 40%

00:47:10.360 --> 00:47:12.100
of my brain activity is focused

00:47:12.100 --> 00:47:13.750
on what this guy thinks of me.

00:47:13.750 --> 00:47:15.910
And it isn't even in the
context of what his rating

00:47:15.910 --> 00:47:17.140
is gonna be of me.

00:47:17.140 --> 00:47:22.008
It's literally like does
guy gonna think I'm a jerk

00:47:22.008 --> 00:47:23.960
because I'm on my phone the whole time

00:47:23.960 --> 00:47:28.300
or is he gonna think that
I'm addicted to my phone?

00:47:28.300 --> 00:47:29.710
Like why do I care?

00:47:29.710 --> 00:47:33.610
Who cares?
Even saying nice things

00:47:33.610 --> 00:47:37.710
is not really about being
nice and maybe this is the key

00:47:37.710 --> 00:47:40.730
to unlocking some new
practice is the realization

00:47:40.730 --> 00:47:45.730
that our niceties are much more about us

00:47:46.350 --> 00:47:49.450
and about shaping perception than it is

00:47:49.450 --> 00:47:52.420
about actually being nice to somebody,

00:47:52.420 --> 00:47:53.540
being kind to someone.

00:47:53.540 --> 00:47:56.230
I think a lot of it is just rooted in ego.

00:47:56.230 --> 00:47:57.233
Yeah.
I do.

00:47:57.233 --> 00:48:00.795
I think there is, this
very human things to do,

00:48:00.795 --> 00:48:03.540
but I think that one of the reasons that

00:48:03.540 --> 00:48:07.040
sometimes you may
interpret an older person

00:48:07.040 --> 00:48:12.040
as being blunt or not nice,
sometimes they are just jerks,

00:48:13.180 --> 00:48:17.770
but I think in a lot of
cases it's that they have

00:48:17.770 --> 00:48:20.820
actually lost some of their
ego as they've gotten older

00:48:20.820 --> 00:48:23.210
which if you're healthy
I think is the direction

00:48:23.210 --> 00:48:24.330
that you should be heading in,

00:48:24.330 --> 00:48:26.180
you're getting less
and less about yourself

00:48:26.180 --> 00:48:29.249
and more and more about
others as you get older,

00:48:29.249 --> 00:48:32.310
and so I do think that,

00:48:32.310 --> 00:48:35.250
and I like to use the word candidness

00:48:35.250 --> 00:48:38.240
because I do think that
sometimes that's what it is,

00:48:38.240 --> 00:48:41.690
it's just like this person
told me what they thought.

00:48:41.690 --> 00:48:43.630
And again, it's not being super,

00:48:43.630 --> 00:48:46.730
I do think that sometimes
being super particular

00:48:46.730 --> 00:48:50.260
about things and then expecting
everybody to fall in line,

00:48:50.260 --> 00:48:52.260
that can become an annoying thing, right?

00:48:52.260 --> 00:48:54.010
But I don't think that's
what asking somebody

00:48:54.010 --> 00:48:57.490
to adjust the temperature if
you're hot in an Uber is about.

00:48:57.490 --> 00:48:59.830
I think it's like well I kinda
don't wanna be burning up

00:48:59.830 --> 00:49:01.880
back here and I know
that this person probably

00:49:01.880 --> 00:49:04.010
actually does care about
what temperature it is.

00:49:04.010 --> 00:49:07.250
Some drivers ask you is it
okay, is it okay back there?

00:49:07.250 --> 00:49:11.550
So it's reasonable and not
jerky to say something about it.

00:49:11.550 --> 00:49:14.790
But I think that a big part of it is just

00:49:14.790 --> 00:49:18.130
reputation management with people

00:49:18.130 --> 00:49:21.190
that you'll never see
again which is weird.

00:49:21.190 --> 00:49:22.880
Yeah.
But it's just this

00:49:22.880 --> 00:49:27.520
wanting so badly to be liked
and to not ruffle feathers

00:49:27.520 --> 00:49:30.973
that you end up doing
a disservice to people.

00:49:32.170 --> 00:49:34.290
And I do think that we--
And yourself

00:49:34.290 --> 00:49:36.760
'cause you're blazin' up in the backseat.

00:49:36.760 --> 00:49:38.810
I think that's a term for smoking weed

00:49:38.810 --> 00:49:39.960
and that's not what I mean.

00:49:39.960 --> 00:49:41.780
Right, yeah yeah.

00:49:41.780 --> 00:49:44.040
But I think there's also a
button for that on the app.

00:49:44.040 --> 00:49:45.230
My name is D-Nice.

00:49:45.230 --> 00:49:47.463
I'm gonna blaze up, just be ready.

00:49:48.800 --> 00:49:50.743
I'm a conversationalist blazer.

00:49:51.850 --> 00:49:55.750
I've never thought about that
but I have thought about,

00:49:55.750 --> 00:49:58.550
can I just bring Jade in an Uber?

00:49:58.550 --> 00:49:59.580
This is just a tangent

00:49:59.580 --> 00:50:02.070
but I was wondering that this morning.

00:50:02.070 --> 00:50:04.280
And again for niceness I was like--

00:50:04.280 --> 00:50:05.113
I would think so.

00:50:05.113 --> 00:50:06.400
I don't want a negative rating

00:50:06.400 --> 00:50:08.850
'cause this whole rating
thing is part of it too.

00:50:10.020 --> 00:50:12.683
It's a quantified version
of what we're talking about.

00:50:12.683 --> 00:50:16.070
It's like I'm gonna treat
you in a way not that I want

00:50:16.070 --> 00:50:18.750
to be treated or just
because you're another human

00:50:18.750 --> 00:50:19.850
that I wanna be nice to,

00:50:19.850 --> 00:50:22.680
I wanna treat you in a way
so that you will give me

00:50:22.680 --> 00:50:23.713
a good rating.

00:50:24.670 --> 00:50:25.830
That's messed up, man.

00:50:25.830 --> 00:50:28.190
I literally think about my rating on Uber.

00:50:28.190 --> 00:50:29.117
I have no clue what it is

00:50:29.117 --> 00:50:31.610
and I don't think it has
any bearing on anything.

00:50:31.610 --> 00:50:33.550
I don't know when it comes into play

00:50:33.550 --> 00:50:36.290
and I don't know when people
can come to a conclusion

00:50:36.290 --> 00:50:37.642
about it, I don't necessarily--

00:50:37.642 --> 00:50:39.730
But if Jade was just sittin' in my lap,

00:50:39.730 --> 00:50:41.860
she'd be here right now.

00:50:41.860 --> 00:50:44.420
I don't think it would be a problem.

00:50:44.420 --> 00:50:47.650
I guess there are some Uber, maybe all--

00:50:47.650 --> 00:50:48.890
Plenty of people who'd have a problem

00:50:48.890 --> 00:50:49.940
with a dog in their car.

00:50:49.940 --> 00:50:53.410
Maybe all ride share operators
have to be okay with dogs.

00:50:53.410 --> 00:50:55.430
Maybe they indicate that
somewhere on the app.

00:50:55.430 --> 00:50:58.250
Maybe, I don't know, it's a good question.

00:50:58.250 --> 00:50:59.190
You don't know the ins and outs--

00:50:59.190 --> 00:51:04.060
Especially with, Jade is not a giant dog.

00:51:04.060 --> 00:51:05.903
If you have a Great Dane
or something like that

00:51:05.903 --> 00:51:07.090
that could be a problem.
I could keep her hidden,

00:51:07.090 --> 00:51:08.740
wouldn't even know she was there.

00:51:09.831 --> 00:51:14.831
Yeah so, what about niceness
when it comes to content

00:51:15.060 --> 00:51:19.507
because the stuff that we create,

00:51:19.507 --> 00:51:23.760
there are entire channels built on,

00:51:23.760 --> 00:51:27.250
well, of course there's entire
channels built on drama.

00:51:27.250 --> 00:51:30.890
I'm not really, I'm not
talking about drama as much as,

00:51:30.890 --> 00:51:35.610
I don't know, I guess this
is a bordering territory

00:51:35.610 --> 00:51:38.790
to it that I'll just
call the hot takes, man.

00:51:38.790 --> 00:51:40.613
You've got an opinion on something.

00:51:41.596 --> 00:51:44.180
So much and it's not just digital content

00:51:44.180 --> 00:51:49.157
but radio for years, television,
all types of talk content

00:51:50.760 --> 00:51:54.963
is, we can talk about it as
it relates to Ear Biscuits,

00:51:56.730 --> 00:52:01.730
is successful because
it's just, it's hot takes.

00:52:02.360 --> 00:52:07.133
It's opinions, I mean and a
lot of times there's a lot of,

00:52:09.110 --> 00:52:12.470
it's super charged with
some vitriol, some anger,

00:52:12.470 --> 00:52:14.400
some bitterness, some

00:52:18.730 --> 00:52:22.080
complaints, the opposite of being nice.

00:52:22.080 --> 00:52:27.080
Being brutally honest or
just having a punchy opinion.

00:52:28.650 --> 00:52:29.730
And that's not...

00:52:31.610 --> 00:52:34.720
Maybe we started to do a little
bit of that but not really.

00:52:34.720 --> 00:52:38.560
I think that when we, on
Ear Biscuits, we kinda...

00:52:40.500 --> 00:52:43.960
We resist the urge to give
our unfettered opinions

00:52:43.960 --> 00:52:45.600
on things because,

00:52:45.600 --> 00:52:48.660
do you think there's a
niceness component there?

00:52:48.660 --> 00:52:50.260
What's going on there?

00:52:50.260 --> 00:52:52.420
Are we too nice when it
comes to our content?

00:52:52.420 --> 00:52:56.313
Well I think that's part of it.

00:52:58.440 --> 00:53:00.620
I think it's really
complex because I do think

00:53:00.620 --> 00:53:01.510
that there is just something

00:53:01.510 --> 00:53:04.630
about our personalities
naturally whether it's niceness

00:53:04.630 --> 00:53:06.380
or however you would wanna describe it

00:53:06.380 --> 00:53:09.470
that we don't tend to be,

00:53:09.470 --> 00:53:12.590
we're both opinionated
for sure but when it comes

00:53:12.590 --> 00:53:15.010
to the types of opinions and hot takes

00:53:15.010 --> 00:53:18.225
that would be the kinda thing that would,

00:53:18.225 --> 00:53:20.520
people would care about on the internet,

00:53:20.520 --> 00:53:23.080
that's not really a natural
part of our dialogue.

00:53:23.080 --> 00:53:24.980
We don't spend a lot of
time thinking and talking

00:53:24.980 --> 00:53:28.260
about those things but I also think that

00:53:29.341 --> 00:53:34.341
because we were so focused
on just making people laugh

00:53:35.240 --> 00:53:39.280
and for a number of reasons
and we'll probably do a podcast

00:53:39.280 --> 00:53:42.467
on this at some point, why
we approached our content

00:53:42.467 --> 00:53:44.090
the way we did in the early days

00:53:44.090 --> 00:53:49.090
and why it started so family friendly,

00:53:50.086 --> 00:53:52.563
more family friendly than it is now.

00:53:53.850 --> 00:53:56.550
Still generally family
friendly but it was like

00:53:56.550 --> 00:53:59.960
squeaky clean G-rated
from the very beginning.

00:53:59.960 --> 00:54:01.050
Yeah.
I think you attract

00:54:01.050 --> 00:54:01.930
a certain audience it's like,

00:54:01.930 --> 00:54:04.903
oh these guys are gonna something
that never offends people

00:54:04.903 --> 00:54:07.800
and it's just all about being lighthearted

00:54:07.800 --> 00:54:10.200
and then when you build
a fan base around that,

00:54:10.200 --> 00:54:13.020
you've kinda cornered
yourself a little bit,

00:54:13.020 --> 00:54:17.140
whereas, one of the things that Harley

00:54:17.140 --> 00:54:19.740
from the Epic Mealtime said,
probably at this table,

00:54:20.713 --> 00:54:25.140
was that you kinda get what
you give to your audience.

00:54:25.140 --> 00:54:28.680
You get back what you give
and he gives his tone,

00:54:28.680 --> 00:54:32.850
especially at the time was
very harsh towards his audience

00:54:32.850 --> 00:54:35.370
and you kind of attract people
who like that kinda thing

00:54:35.370 --> 00:54:36.980
and then they kinda give it back.

00:54:36.980 --> 00:54:39.110
So I think that we've actually developed

00:54:39.110 --> 00:54:42.170
a pretty nice community

00:54:43.460 --> 00:54:46.800
because the content isn't
vitriolic in any way.

00:54:46.800 --> 00:54:48.610
And we're proud of that.
Right.

00:54:48.610 --> 00:54:49.620
That is something to be proud of.

00:54:49.620 --> 00:54:52.020
That's a good thing.
But even me saying that

00:54:52.020 --> 00:54:56.420
right now is like oh I
don't want it to seem like

00:54:56.420 --> 00:55:01.420
I'm being critical of our
core audience or an aspect

00:55:01.860 --> 00:55:05.403
of our, a contingent of
our audience's taste.

00:55:06.410 --> 00:55:10.620
Well here's a perfect example of--

00:55:10.620 --> 00:55:11.710
'Cause I'm not being critical of you.

00:55:11.710 --> 00:55:14.830
Where I think that the
audience that we cultivated

00:55:17.230 --> 00:55:21.680
and how nice they were and
are, for the most part,

00:55:21.680 --> 00:55:24.350
created a content problem for us

00:55:24.350 --> 00:55:26.240
and that is with our
local commercials, right,

00:55:26.240 --> 00:55:27.870
we've talked about this a number of times.

00:55:27.870 --> 00:55:30.041
Yeah that's a good case study.

00:55:30.041 --> 00:55:33.440
We've never really been
into prank content per se.

00:55:33.440 --> 00:55:35.450
We did that one prank video.

00:55:35.450 --> 00:55:37.750
We've done a number of pranks like all

00:55:37.750 --> 00:55:39.690
of our man on the street videos--

00:55:39.690 --> 00:55:40.610
Yeah they were--

00:55:40.610 --> 00:55:43.410
Were--
They're kinda prank-inspired

00:55:43.410 --> 00:55:45.550
and they're kinda--
Made people feel awkward

00:55:45.550 --> 00:55:47.300
at least.
They're based on making

00:55:47.300 --> 00:55:49.830
normal people feel a
little bit out of sorts

00:55:49.830 --> 00:55:51.830
and getting some laughs at their expense.

00:55:51.830 --> 00:55:55.380
Not in a super mean-spirited
way but for some people,

00:55:55.380 --> 00:55:57.310
anything in that realm is mean-spirited.

00:55:57.310 --> 00:55:59.630
And the nature of the local
commercials that we made back

00:55:59.630 --> 00:56:01.580
in the day and then on Commercial Kings,

00:56:01.580 --> 00:56:04.197
which is still back in the
day, this is a long time ago,

00:56:04.197 --> 00:56:06.720
of course we used real
businesses and real people

00:56:06.720 --> 00:56:09.589
from those businesses and the
thing that we found so funny

00:56:09.589 --> 00:56:12.870
is when we put normal people
into these situations,

00:56:12.870 --> 00:56:15.830
these ridiculous structures
that we created for them

00:56:15.830 --> 00:56:17.520
and they just did their thing.

00:56:17.520 --> 00:56:20.330
You're kinda laughing at slash with them,

00:56:20.330 --> 00:56:22.690
and everybody ends up being
happy and it's good for business

00:56:22.690 --> 00:56:26.863
and people love the result
but one of the reasons that--

00:56:26.863 --> 00:56:28.310
It's not clear-cut though.

00:56:28.310 --> 00:56:31.510
A lot of people are
uncomfortable with it is because

00:56:31.510 --> 00:56:34.229
a really, really strict
interpretation of it is that

00:56:34.229 --> 00:56:36.170
you're laughing at these people,

00:56:36.170 --> 00:56:40.050
you're having fun and
humor at their expense.

00:56:40.050 --> 00:56:42.760
And some people are not
comfortable with that at all.

00:56:42.760 --> 00:56:46.523
So now, we are both very
comfortable with that.

00:56:46.523 --> 00:56:48.872
It's a little bit of a generational thing.

00:56:48.872 --> 00:56:51.100
I do think that the younger generation is

00:56:51.100 --> 00:56:54.180
much less comfortable with
it although I will say

00:56:54.180 --> 00:56:57.030
that even though I didn't encourage this,

00:56:57.030 --> 00:57:01.110
my kids are much like I was at their age.

00:57:01.110 --> 00:57:04.090
Some people can't watch the
show that we talked about,

00:57:04.090 --> 00:57:05.720
Impractical Jokers, some
people can't watch that

00:57:05.720 --> 00:57:06.920
'cause they're like I just don't like

00:57:06.920 --> 00:57:08.810
to see anybody being made
to feel uncomfortable.

00:57:08.810 --> 00:57:10.170
They love it.

00:57:10.170 --> 00:57:12.120
They think it's great.

00:57:12.120 --> 00:57:15.840
But we ended up stopping
doing those commercials

00:57:15.840 --> 00:57:19.730
because we just ended up
getting, one of the reasons

00:57:19.730 --> 00:57:21.970
was we just ended up
getting so much push-back

00:57:21.970 --> 00:57:23.980
'cause people would be like,
this doesn't seem like,

00:57:23.980 --> 00:57:26.490
this isn't the stuff
that I like you guys for.

00:57:26.490 --> 00:57:28.300
This just seems a little bit mean.

00:57:28.300 --> 00:57:30.100
Mean-spirited.
And so we just were like

00:57:30.100 --> 00:57:32.230
okay well, it's not like
we have to keep doing this.

00:57:32.230 --> 00:57:35.116
We did a bunch of it, we can
move onto something else.

00:57:35.116 --> 00:57:39.759
So I do think that that is
an example of our niceness

00:57:39.759 --> 00:57:43.080
in general and especially
in things like GMM,

00:57:43.080 --> 00:57:46.780
cultivating an audience that
then couldn't really take

00:57:46.780 --> 00:57:49.210
the non-nice content.

00:57:49.210 --> 00:57:52.570
Yeah and I was talking
to Arin from Game Grumps

00:57:52.570 --> 00:57:56.910
and he was talking about
his, their talk show,

00:57:56.910 --> 00:58:01.910
which is inspired format-wise
by Good Mythical Morning.

00:58:01.950 --> 00:58:03.900
The 10 Minute Power Hour.

00:58:03.900 --> 00:58:05.563
Which we're big fans of.

00:58:07.300 --> 00:58:09.823
And he was talking about how, he said,

00:58:11.104 --> 00:58:13.696
he was talking about the GMM influence

00:58:13.696 --> 00:58:17.400
on how they created that but
he was like, but we've got,

00:58:17.400 --> 00:58:19.680
the tone is a little different
'cause we got a bit more

00:58:19.680 --> 00:58:21.940
of that eff you energy.

00:58:21.940 --> 00:58:25.450
You guys don't really have eff you energy

00:58:25.450 --> 00:58:28.706
and I was saying something
about their Reddit contingent

00:58:28.706 --> 00:58:31.093
and he was like,

00:58:33.220 --> 00:58:38.220
the people that respond to
that are the people who then,

00:58:38.640 --> 00:58:40.830
they'll like eff you in the comments

00:58:40.830 --> 00:58:44.383
but then when we looked at how
much merch we were selling,

00:58:46.223 --> 00:58:49.913
they have a whole Reddit thread that them

00:58:51.400 --> 00:58:53.340
or none of their crew
has anything to do with

00:58:53.340 --> 00:58:58.340
because it's just all the
fans who just hate on them.

00:58:59.484 --> 00:59:02.240
But he said that a remarkable percentage,

00:59:02.240 --> 00:59:03.260
of course I don't remember the number

00:59:03.260 --> 00:59:05.520
'cause I don't have mental capacity

00:59:05.520 --> 00:59:06.840
to remember other people's numbers.

00:59:06.840 --> 00:59:08.236
Right, you're thinking
about your vacation.

00:59:10.510 --> 00:59:13.730
He said that it was a big
contingent of merch sales

00:59:13.730 --> 00:59:17.190
are from those people who are
constantly eff you, eff you.

00:59:17.190 --> 00:59:20.210
Because they're very
invested and they're fans.

00:59:20.210 --> 00:59:21.709
It's just--
It's a different way

00:59:21.709 --> 00:59:23.840
of expressing--
You get what you give

00:59:23.840 --> 00:59:28.750
and I'm a little envious of that energy

00:59:28.750 --> 00:59:32.700
because I don't know,
from a comedic standpoint,

00:59:32.700 --> 00:59:37.390
it seems, I don't know what the word is,

00:59:37.390 --> 00:59:40.650
it just seems more emphatic.

00:59:40.650 --> 00:59:44.490
Comedically emphatic, when
you're doing something

00:59:44.490 --> 00:59:47.593
that can be, is edgy,

00:59:49.771 --> 00:59:53.100
quite literally edgy so it
pushes you either one way

00:59:53.100 --> 00:59:54.693
or the other, right?

00:59:57.300 --> 01:00:00.880
It's easy to subconsciously feel like

01:00:00.880 --> 01:00:02.820
that's a more legitimate form of comedy

01:00:02.820 --> 01:00:06.040
because it's sending you
in one of two directions,

01:00:06.040 --> 01:00:08.600
either love or hate versus just like...

01:00:11.150 --> 01:00:16.150
I think our tone, it's very welcoming.

01:00:17.910 --> 01:00:22.260
Oh I can tolerate it, as
where someone may be left.

01:00:22.260 --> 01:00:24.990
And I think that we end
up compensating for it

01:00:24.990 --> 01:00:27.050
a little bit because again,

01:00:27.050 --> 01:00:29.130
if we were just completely ourselves,

01:00:29.130 --> 01:00:30.680
throw caution to the wind,

01:00:30.680 --> 01:00:33.410
we would be more offensive than we are.

01:00:33.410 --> 01:00:37.010
If there was no guard up at all as to

01:00:37.010 --> 01:00:41.570
what is a more broadly appealing,
welcoming sort of tone,

01:00:41.570 --> 01:00:43.910
it would be a more caustic tone.

01:00:43.910 --> 01:00:45.000
It just would be.

01:00:45.000 --> 01:00:47.260
Of course we have more
opinions than we share.

01:00:47.260 --> 01:00:49.340
Yeah and also--
And I know that people

01:00:49.340 --> 01:00:51.640
are gonna say they want more of that.

01:00:51.640 --> 01:00:52.968
Well and you might think that--

01:00:52.968 --> 01:00:53.934
Now that we're talking about it.

01:00:53.934 --> 01:00:55.698
You might just say well
I can see that coming

01:00:55.698 --> 01:00:58.360
from Rhett but from
Link, no, the reality is,

01:00:58.360 --> 01:01:00.940
it would come from both of,
I don't wanna speak for you.

01:01:00.940 --> 01:01:03.710
Yeah, no--
It's not just a thing

01:01:03.710 --> 01:01:05.943
that comes from the bitter tall one.

01:01:07.240 --> 01:01:12.000
But interestingly, you're
also kinda talking about

01:01:14.310 --> 01:01:15.940
stand-up comedy as an example,

01:01:15.940 --> 01:01:19.088
the purest form of comedy, right?

01:01:19.088 --> 01:01:21.670
Which we've never really, we do it kind of

01:01:21.670 --> 01:01:23.840
but we haven't done traditional stand-up,

01:01:23.840 --> 01:01:25.590
definitely not alone.
I thought farts were

01:01:25.590 --> 01:01:27.750
the purest form of comedy.

01:01:27.750 --> 01:01:29.310
Go ahead.

01:01:29.310 --> 01:01:30.220
Are you saying that because my seat

01:01:30.220 --> 01:01:31.570
just made a farting noise?

01:01:31.570 --> 01:01:33.070
No I was just making a joke.

01:01:34.697 --> 01:01:37.440
The whole give and take that
you have with an audience

01:01:37.440 --> 01:01:39.856
and the fact that if
you're going to speak up

01:01:39.856 --> 01:01:44.397
to a comedian, he or she is
going to rip you a new one

01:01:44.397 --> 01:01:46.650
and that's part of the act.

01:01:46.650 --> 01:01:48.820
Heckling and then responding
to hecklers is part

01:01:48.820 --> 01:01:50.910
of being a great stand-up and--

01:01:50.910 --> 01:01:52.350
A certain type of stand-up though.

01:01:52.350 --> 01:01:54.128
I don't think that
applies to all stand-up.

01:01:54.128 --> 01:01:58.000
Well no no, even the nicest comedian.

01:01:58.000 --> 01:01:59.150
Like a Brian Regan.

01:01:59.150 --> 01:02:01.000
Even the nicest comedian.

01:02:01.000 --> 01:02:03.070
A Gaffigan.
Is not going, Gaffigan,

01:02:03.070 --> 01:02:07.160
okay, is not, I guarantee
you that when he does

01:02:07.160 --> 01:02:09.230
his live stand-up and somebody heckles him

01:02:09.230 --> 01:02:10.600
that he gets the best of them.

01:02:10.600 --> 01:02:13.040
Oh yeah.
Because you can't exist.

01:02:13.040 --> 01:02:14.653
How mean-spirited is it?

01:02:17.380 --> 01:02:20.070
We kinda do that on tour.

01:02:20.070 --> 01:02:24.197
We don't do anything that's
ridiculously vindictive

01:02:25.410 --> 01:02:27.560
or whatever but if you're
gonna have the courage

01:02:27.560 --> 01:02:30.100
to speak up at our tour, we're
gonna end up doing something

01:02:30.100 --> 01:02:32.143
that's gonna make people laugh at you.

01:02:33.040 --> 01:02:34.710
But it's all in good fun.

01:02:34.710 --> 01:02:36.358
I definitely don't wanna
get into a place where

01:02:36.358 --> 01:02:38.560
we're not willing to just,

01:02:38.560 --> 01:02:40.668
light ribbing is no longer allowed.

01:02:40.668 --> 01:02:45.668
But how, I think the question is is like,

01:02:46.150 --> 01:02:48.920
how does it work its way into the content?

01:02:48.920 --> 01:02:51.520
What are we afraid of?

01:02:51.520 --> 01:02:54.470
And how do you navigate it?

01:02:54.470 --> 01:02:56.480
Because just another quick data point is

01:02:56.480 --> 01:02:59.180
when we were guests on the H3 Podcast.

01:02:59.180 --> 01:03:02.830
Big fans of Ethan and Hila
and their growing family

01:03:04.890 --> 01:03:07.960
of sons and dogs, well
they've got one of each.

01:03:07.960 --> 01:03:08.793
Yeah.
It's like,

01:03:08.793 --> 01:03:10.943
it's hot take central over there.

01:03:11.882 --> 01:03:14.043
And it's extremely entertaining.

01:03:17.100 --> 01:03:21.490
It's hot takes for the
sake of entertainment.

01:03:21.490 --> 01:03:23.130
That's why I enjoyed it.

01:03:23.130 --> 01:03:26.150
It's almost like taking a
position just for the laughs

01:03:26.150 --> 01:03:29.403
a lot of time, not all the time.

01:03:30.420 --> 01:03:32.070
And so being on there was like...

01:03:34.610 --> 01:03:37.140
I think when it comes to the
type of things they talk about,

01:03:37.140 --> 01:03:39.230
you're more informed and more opinionated

01:03:39.230 --> 01:03:42.880
about those specific
things so that felt like

01:03:42.880 --> 01:03:45.190
more of an outlet for you to be there

01:03:45.190 --> 01:03:47.033
than it did for me.

01:03:49.750 --> 01:03:52.550
I think there were other
things that would get me going.

01:03:53.560 --> 01:03:54.803
Probably germs.

01:03:57.439 --> 01:04:00.300
Or herky jerky Uber drivers.

01:04:01.370 --> 01:04:04.150
But the type of stuff they
talk about was something that,

01:04:04.150 --> 01:04:07.240
I mean we don't go into
that stuff on our podcast.

01:04:07.240 --> 01:04:10.730
Smosh does some of that on their podcast.

01:04:10.730 --> 01:04:15.730
Because it is a great
way to develop a fan base

01:04:15.790 --> 01:04:17.820
if that's something, if
that's a way you're interested

01:04:17.820 --> 01:04:19.680
in talking about things.

01:04:19.680 --> 01:04:20.820
But it's...

01:04:23.470 --> 01:04:26.110
I don't, you tell me what you think.

01:04:26.110 --> 01:04:28.053
I don't think that's a place where,

01:04:29.190 --> 01:04:31.800
we've made a decision
that that's not what,

01:04:31.800 --> 01:04:33.800
that's not the type of place that we go.

01:04:34.794 --> 01:04:37.770
And I'm not talking about
YouTube drama as much as

01:04:38.980 --> 01:04:41.653
current event hot take
type stuff where it's,

01:04:43.345 --> 01:04:46.080
you cannot be nice.

01:04:46.080 --> 01:04:47.970
You cannot come across
as nice, that's not part

01:04:47.970 --> 01:04:50.900
of the equation in those conversations.

01:04:50.900 --> 01:04:54.810
Well, I feel like trying

01:04:54.810 --> 01:04:59.810
to incorporate drama or commentary

01:05:00.590 --> 01:05:05.590
about controversial things
into what we're currently doing

01:05:07.108 --> 01:05:09.203
would be a little bit of a betrayal.

01:05:11.228 --> 01:05:16.228
Because I feel like there's
so much of that, right?

01:05:16.861 --> 01:05:17.790
All out there.

01:05:17.790 --> 01:05:21.480
We're not just throwing what
we create into a vacuum.

01:05:21.480 --> 01:05:24.640
It is experienced in the
greater context of internet

01:05:24.640 --> 01:05:26.780
and entertainment in general.

01:05:26.780 --> 01:05:29.530
And we are so polarized right now, right?

01:05:29.530 --> 01:05:33.730
So as opinionated as I am

01:05:33.730 --> 01:05:38.730
and as bitter as I can be

01:05:38.742 --> 01:05:40.493
about some things,

01:05:41.370 --> 01:05:43.320
I do think there's something to be said

01:05:44.170 --> 01:05:49.170
for the fact that our show
and our content in general

01:05:50.730 --> 01:05:55.370
is something people can
come together over, around.

01:05:55.370 --> 01:05:57.990
People of differing backgrounds,

01:05:57.990 --> 01:06:00.740
people of differing political opinions

01:06:01.770 --> 01:06:04.100
should be able to come
to our show and be like

01:06:04.100 --> 01:06:05.690
this is something that we can agree on.

01:06:05.690 --> 01:06:07.100
And I do think there's power in that.

01:06:07.100 --> 01:06:08.210
Some people call it a cop-out,

01:06:08.210 --> 01:06:10.340
some people say you're doing
that just so you can make

01:06:10.340 --> 01:06:13.160
more money and have a
broadly appealing product.

01:06:13.160 --> 01:06:16.640
I actually personally and I think I speak

01:06:16.640 --> 01:06:19.283
for both of us but I know
that that's something

01:06:19.283 --> 01:06:23.500
that I care about is I
don't like the disunity

01:06:23.500 --> 01:06:25.000
in our world and in our country.

01:06:25.000 --> 01:06:26.860
It's needed now more than ever.

01:06:26.860 --> 01:06:30.300
And I think that--
Something that brings

01:06:30.300 --> 01:06:31.510
us together.
And I do think

01:06:31.510 --> 01:06:35.998
that we're kind of especially equipped

01:06:35.998 --> 01:06:38.223
to kinda bring people together.

01:06:39.450 --> 01:06:43.740
We understand multiple perspectives,

01:06:43.740 --> 01:06:47.100
having embodied multiple
perspectives over the course

01:06:47.100 --> 01:06:50.600
of our lives and I think that that puts us

01:06:50.600 --> 01:06:53.390
in a position to be like hey,
all these people aren't bad

01:06:53.390 --> 01:06:54.870
and all these people aren't bad.

01:06:54.870 --> 01:06:58.160
It's like hey, let's have a dialogue.

01:06:58.160 --> 01:06:59.620
There are some things
that we can agree on.

01:06:59.620 --> 01:07:01.140
Now we don't end up talking directly

01:07:01.140 --> 01:07:02.763
about those things very often.

01:07:04.686 --> 01:07:06.230
So I do think it would be
a little bit of disservice

01:07:06.230 --> 01:07:08.560
to be like all of a sudden
we're gonna be talking

01:07:08.560 --> 01:07:10.270
about all this stuff and
we're gonna be throwing

01:07:10.270 --> 01:07:13.620
our opinions out there and
blasting people who disagree.

01:07:13.620 --> 01:07:15.430
That's just not the spirit of what we do.

01:07:15.430 --> 01:07:18.360
But I do think the thing
that, one of the reasons

01:07:18.360 --> 01:07:22.350
that what Ethan and Hila do is appealing

01:07:22.350 --> 01:07:24.960
or what Game Grumps does
is appealing is because

01:07:24.960 --> 01:07:27.720
it's more fun to just let your guard down.

01:07:27.720 --> 01:07:30.530
It's more fun to just
be honest and be like

01:07:30.530 --> 01:07:32.792
I don't really care about the
impact or the consequences

01:07:32.792 --> 01:07:34.480
because I'm just being myself.

01:07:34.480 --> 01:07:37.050
To have a little filter and a
guard on everything you say,

01:07:37.050 --> 01:07:39.203
it's a little bit tiresome so I do think

01:07:39.203 --> 01:07:41.830
that as you get older
and I think this is one

01:07:41.830 --> 01:07:46.020
of the reason that there is
more innuendo as an example

01:07:46.020 --> 01:07:48.600
in all of our content now
than there was when we started

01:07:48.600 --> 01:07:51.623
is because you know what,
when we talk privately,

01:07:52.650 --> 01:07:53.933
it's a lot of innuendo.

01:07:55.570 --> 01:07:59.590
We both find sexual
innuendo to be very funny

01:07:59.590 --> 01:08:03.410
and we use a lot of it in
our normal private lives.

01:08:03.410 --> 01:08:04.930
So--
We also did when we were

01:08:04.930 --> 01:08:06.630
in seventh grade.
We've always done it.

01:08:06.630 --> 01:08:09.230
Do you remember--
From the very beginning.

01:08:09.230 --> 01:08:11.290
But we haven't done it in our content.

01:08:11.290 --> 01:08:12.210
Right.

01:08:12.210 --> 01:08:15.264
Until it's 1500, 1600 episodes,

01:08:15.264 --> 01:08:18.530
you just come a little bit more yourself

01:08:18.530 --> 01:08:20.880
and I think that there's
something freeing about that.

01:08:20.880 --> 01:08:24.140
And that's a place, I think
the sexual innuendo's a place

01:08:24.140 --> 01:08:27.387
where we've, we've talked about it some

01:08:27.387 --> 01:08:32.373
and we continue to talk
about the ways that we,

01:08:33.858 --> 01:08:38.840
the lines, the ways that we
will incorporate innuendo

01:08:38.840 --> 01:08:43.580
and the ways we won't so we
know what our best practices are

01:08:43.580 --> 01:08:45.340
to use the corporate term.

01:08:45.340 --> 01:08:49.500
But in an effort to
conclude this conversation,

01:08:49.500 --> 01:08:51.160
I think that it's...

01:08:53.330 --> 01:08:55.120
I need to have a prolonged thought.

01:08:55.120 --> 01:08:56.430
We've got time.

01:08:56.430 --> 01:08:58.140
Yeah in an effort to come to a conclusion,

01:08:58.140 --> 01:09:03.140
I think that we can, I
feel good with the summary

01:09:06.160 --> 01:09:10.110
that you've given of hey,
we're trying to create things

01:09:10.110 --> 01:09:15.110
in conversations where it
can be an outlet for anybody.

01:09:16.880 --> 01:09:18.010
That simple.

01:09:18.010 --> 01:09:20.930
Well I would say that that's a principle

01:09:20.930 --> 01:09:22.740
that applies to our content.

01:09:22.740 --> 01:09:26.880
We don't wanna be a dividing force.

01:09:26.880 --> 01:09:28.900
We don't wanna be a
polarizing force in the world

01:09:28.900 --> 01:09:31.020
because there's enough of that already.

01:09:31.020 --> 01:09:32.640
That doesn't mean that
there are some things

01:09:32.640 --> 01:09:37.070
that we consider to be
non-political but just human issues

01:09:37.070 --> 01:09:39.160
that need to be addressed
that we will take a stand on

01:09:39.160 --> 01:09:43.270
and talk about and that's at
our discretion as we see fit

01:09:43.270 --> 01:09:47.620
but I think that as time goes by,

01:09:47.620 --> 01:09:51.440
we will continue to be
more and more ourselves.

01:09:51.440 --> 01:09:54.990
Yeah I think it's, yeah,
the balancing point

01:09:54.990 --> 01:09:57.573
just to piggyback on that is,

01:09:59.236 --> 01:10:02.300
yeah, we're not gonna be fake.

01:10:02.300 --> 01:10:06.800
We're not gonna be nice and
lie, lie through our teeth

01:10:06.800 --> 01:10:08.517
just to be nice.
Yeah.

01:10:08.517 --> 01:10:12.740
I wanna be nice when I
actually mean what I'm saying.

01:10:12.740 --> 01:10:15.180
I don't wanna say anything
here that I do not mean.

01:10:15.180 --> 01:10:16.338
Exactly.
So I think

01:10:16.338 --> 01:10:18.840
that's why we choose our
topics of conversation.

01:10:18.840 --> 01:10:22.680
And I think that I've
seen, you see the chatter

01:10:22.680 --> 01:10:24.640
and the speculation sometimes
when people are trying

01:10:24.640 --> 01:10:28.860
to figure out what things
lead to the evolution

01:10:28.860 --> 01:10:31.693
of the show over time or the way we talk.

01:10:32.600 --> 01:10:35.090
And I think one of the accusations

01:10:35.090 --> 01:10:37.630
that can be levied sometimes is that oh,

01:10:37.630 --> 01:10:39.880
you guys are continuing
to push the boundaries

01:10:39.880 --> 01:10:43.120
because you want your
content to be more accepted

01:10:43.120 --> 01:10:47.040
or you're doing this to get more attention

01:10:47.040 --> 01:10:48.605
or to get people to care about you

01:10:48.605 --> 01:10:52.730
and I think that, well,
the reality is is that

01:10:52.730 --> 01:10:55.417
we've kinda always talked this way

01:10:55.417 --> 01:10:59.680
but the longer we do it, the
more the layers get pulled back

01:10:59.680 --> 01:11:01.033
and you see who we are.

01:11:02.420 --> 01:11:04.270
And personally that's something that,

01:11:05.260 --> 01:11:07.260
I've been focusing on
this personally a lot

01:11:07.260 --> 01:11:08.530
just through therapy honestly,

01:11:08.530 --> 01:11:11.690
it's just getting out of my
head, getting into my heart,

01:11:11.690 --> 01:11:16.050
living more from a place of feeling

01:11:16.050 --> 01:11:18.240
because I've done enough thinking.

01:11:18.240 --> 01:11:20.384
I've done a lot of thinking.

01:11:20.384 --> 01:11:24.060
And I think part of that is
continuing to kinda let go

01:11:24.060 --> 01:11:26.020
of what people think about you,

01:11:26.020 --> 01:11:28.820
continuing to have your
ego hopefully continue

01:11:28.820 --> 01:11:31.030
to be drained as you get older.

01:11:31.030 --> 01:11:34.500
And I think just a natural result of that

01:11:34.500 --> 01:11:36.840
is that you're just
gonna be more of yourself

01:11:36.840 --> 01:11:40.700
and so the things that
we do aren't a result

01:11:40.700 --> 01:11:43.590
of trying to be provocative,
of trying to get more views,

01:11:43.590 --> 01:11:45.300
we're always going to be
trying to get more views,

01:11:45.300 --> 01:11:48.750
we're trying to be successful
but it's really us being more

01:11:48.750 --> 01:11:50.770
of ourselves and I think that ultimately

01:11:50.770 --> 01:11:52.410
to tie it back to the original question,

01:11:52.410 --> 01:11:55.730
it's that we're not as
nice as you think we are

01:11:55.730 --> 01:11:57.480
if you thought we were really nice.

01:11:58.945 --> 01:12:01.820
And that's a broad word but I think that

01:12:01.820 --> 01:12:04.840
if some of these things--
But there is a core niceness

01:12:04.840 --> 01:12:09.230
that we're striving to be nice

01:12:09.230 --> 01:12:13.380
as long as it's true to ourselves.

01:12:13.380 --> 01:12:14.213
Yeah.

01:12:15.671 --> 01:12:19.510
#EarBiscuits, let us know what you think.

01:12:19.510 --> 01:12:21.250
Join in on the conversation.

01:12:21.250 --> 01:12:22.463
Recs in Effect.

01:12:23.900 --> 01:12:25.563
I'll give a music rec.

01:12:26.540 --> 01:12:28.200
Been listening to The Bahamas.

01:12:28.200 --> 01:12:31.220
I don't think I've rec'ed Bahamas.

01:12:31.220 --> 01:12:32.760
I think we've talked about it personally

01:12:32.760 --> 01:12:36.140
'cause I'm like, we used to
really be into Jack Johnson,

01:12:36.140 --> 01:12:39.500
that laid-back, you talk about
Roy and his Hawaiian vibe

01:12:39.500 --> 01:12:42.040
with his elevator, the elevator goes slow

01:12:42.040 --> 01:12:45.100
when you're listening to some
Jack Johnson back in the day.

01:12:45.100 --> 01:12:49.440
Bahamas is kinda like
my updated Jack Johnson.

01:12:49.440 --> 01:12:54.243
And the specific song that
I'm gonna recommend is,

01:12:55.100 --> 01:12:57.893
gotta turn, why I gotta turn my,

01:12:59.140 --> 01:13:00.913
gotta take it out of airport mode.

01:13:03.030 --> 01:13:05.030
I know it's airplane mode.

